[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Wrong [38;5;10mdatalen[0m units in [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m calls (hash.rs)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m function takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m (not bytes). In the C code for [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m, the callers pass raw byte counts directly:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m// C code — passes byte counts, which blake256_update interprets as bits
blakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);       // SPX_N = 16 → 16 bits = 2 bytes hashed
blakeX_update(&S, pk, SPX_PK_BYTES);    // 32 → 32 bits = 4 bytes hashed
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation "helpfully" multiplied by 8 to convert to bits:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// WRONG — this hashes 16 bytes instead of 2
blake256_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], (SPX_N as u64) * 8);
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This changes the actual data being hashed, producing completely different output. The fix was to remove the [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications to match the C behavior exactly:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// CORRECT — matches C behavior
blake256_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], SPX_N as u64);
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
